WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.860
Welcome to the Human Physiology data workshop.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.340
Use the question's measurements rather than memorised average values.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.720
If tidal volume is five hundred millilitres per breath and breathing frequency

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.040
is twelve per minute, minute ventilation is six thousand millilitres per

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.840
minute, or six litres per minute.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.220
This measures air moved, not the volume of oxygen taken into blood.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.940
Multiplication cancels the breath unit.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.182
Before comparing two measurements, check whether the volumes describe the

00:00:30.182 --> 00:00:30.420
whole

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.620
breath or only the air reaching gas exchanging regions.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.640
These examples teach model calculations and are not a way to diagnose a person

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.840
from a single reading.

00:00:40.008 --> 00:00:43.918
Suppose the question specifies anatomical dead space of one hundred fifty

00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:44.868
millilitres per breath.

00:00:45.928 --> 00:00:49.388
With a five hundred millilitre tidal volume and twelve breaths per minute,

00:00:50.108 --> 00:00:54.608
estimated alveolar ventilation is three hundred fifty times twelve, or four

00:00:54.608 --> 00:00:55.788
point two litres per minute.

00:00:56.908 --> 00:01:00.268
Compare shallow breathing at three hundred millilitres and twenty breaths per

00:01:00.268 --> 00:01:00.468
minute.

00:01:01.408 --> 00:01:05.528
Minute ventilation is still six litres, but the same assumed dead space leaves

00:01:05.528 --> 00:01:07.948
only three litres per minute of alveolar ventilation.

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:12.848
Therefore equal total air movement does not establish equal effective

00:01:12.848 --> 00:01:13.308
ventilation.

00:01:14.308 --> 00:01:18.468
This simplified calculation holds dead space fixed and describes the supplied

00:01:18.468 --> 00:01:21.708
model, not every real breathing pattern or disease state.

00:01:21.912 --> 00:01:27.872
A problem gives inspiratory reserve volume as three litres, tidal volume as

00:01:27.872 --> 00:01:33.752
half a litre, expiratory reserve volume as one litre and residual volume as

00:01:33.752 --> 00:01:35.932
one point two litres.

00:01:35.932 --> 00:01:40.212
Vital capacity is the first three added together, giving four point five

00:01:40.212 --> 00:01:41.632
litres.

00:01:41.632 --> 00:01:45.972
Total lung capacity also includes residual volume and is five point seven

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:47.112
litres.

00:01:47.112 --> 00:01:52.612
Residual volume remains after maximal expiration, so it is not part of the air

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:54.512
expelled as vital capacity.

00:01:55.692 --> 00:02:00.912
A simple spirometer records exchanged air and cannot directly measure the

00:02:00.912 --> 00:02:01.292
residual volume.

00:02:02.512 --> 00:02:06.352
Consequently capacities containing residual volume require additional

00:02:06.352 --> 00:02:07.192
measurement methods.

00:02:08.372 --> 00:02:11.092
Label each compartment before adding numbers from a graph.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.680
For one ventricle, suppose end diastolic volume is one hundred twenty

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:18.620
millilitres and end systolic volume is fifty.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.740
Stroke volume is seventy millilitres per beat.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:26.960
At seventy five beats per minute, cardiac output is five thousand two hundred

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.960
fifty millilitres per minute, or five point two five litres per minute.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.200
Do not add left and right ventricular outputs to report the ordinary cardiac

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:41.020
output value: the pulmonary and systemic circuits are arranged in series.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:45.280
At a steady state their average outputs are approximately equal.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.252
A change in heart rate alone does not determine the new output if stroke

00:02:50.252 --> 00:02:50.600
volume

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:51.440
also changes.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:51.792
Keep the two factors visible.

00:02:51.792 --> 00:02:56.652
An electrocardiogram records electrical activity rather than blood volume or

00:02:56.652 --> 00:02:57.492
force of contraction.

00:02:58.452 --> 00:03:02.032
In the standard interpretation, the P wave corresponds to atrial

00:03:02.032 --> 00:03:07.812
depolarisation, the Q R S complex to ventricular depolarisation, and the T

00:03:07.812 --> 00:03:11.152
wave to ventricular repolarisation.

00:03:11.152 --> 00:03:14.652
Mechanical events follow electrical activation; they are not identical

00:03:14.652 --> 00:03:15.112
measurements.

00:03:15.852 --> 00:03:20.372
For a regular rhythm with successive R peaks separated by zero point eight

00:03:20.372 --> 00:03:25.572
seconds, the rate is sixty divided by zero point eight, or seventy five beats

00:03:25.572 --> 00:03:26.052
per minute.

00:03:27.112 --> 00:03:28.352
Use a stated time scale.

00:03:29.292 --> 00:03:33.052
For an irregular trace, one interval may not represent the average rate.

00:03:33.672 --> 00:03:38.292
This workshop interprets textbook traces and does not diagnose real ECG

00:03:38.292 --> 00:03:38.732
recordings.

00:03:39.408 --> 00:03:44.112
A hypothetical combined glomerular filtration rate is one hundred twenty five

00:03:44.112 --> 00:03:44.988
millilitres per minute.

00:03:44.988 --> 00:03:49.460
Multiply by one thousand four hundred forty minutes in a day to obtain one

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:53.128
hundred eighty thousand millilitres, or one hundred eighty litres of filtrate

00:03:53.128 --> 00:03:53.608
per day.

00:03:54.108 --> 00:03:56.768
That is not one hundred eighty litres of final urine.

00:03:57.608 --> 00:04:01.288
Tubular processing returns most filtered water and many solutes to the

00:04:01.288 --> 00:04:01.628
circulation.

00:04:02.368 --> 00:04:06.288
If final urine volume in the supplied dataset is one point eight litres per

00:04:06.288 --> 00:04:10.288
day, the net difference in water volumes is one hundred seventy eight point

00:04:10.288 --> 00:04:10.448
two

00:04:10.448 --> 00:04:11.368
litres.

00:04:11.368 --> 00:04:14.688
The simplified fraction returned is ninety nine percent.

00:04:14.688 --> 00:04:19.228
Use the stated values; they are a calculation example rather than a universal

00:04:19.228 --> 00:04:19.988
personal target.

00:04:20.328 --> 00:04:24.715
For a model solute, suppose filtered load is one hundred milligrams per

00:04:24.715 --> 00:04:25.148
minute.

00:04:26.068 --> 00:04:30.528
Tubular secretion adds twenty, while reabsorption removes ninety from the

00:04:30.528 --> 00:04:31.028
tubular fluid.

00:04:32.308 --> 00:04:34.448
Excretion is therefore thirty milligrams per minute.

00:04:35.388 --> 00:04:40.006
Reabsorption moves material from tubular fluid toward blood; secretion moves

00:04:40.006 --> 00:04:40.128
it

00:04:40.128 --> 00:04:41.188
in the opposite direction.

00:04:42.088 --> 00:04:46.068
Do not substitute a concentration in milligrams per millilitre for an amount

00:04:46.068 --> 00:04:46.588
per minute.

00:04:46.588 --> 00:04:52.408
To calculate excretion from urine data, multiply urine concentration by urine

00:04:52.408 --> 00:04:53.788
flow in matching units.

00:04:54.748 --> 00:04:58.868
A urine concentration of ten milligrams per millilitre and flow of three

00:04:58.868 --> 00:05:01.788
millilitres per minute gives thirty milligrams per minute.

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:06.448
Water removal can raise concentration without creating additional solute.

00:05:06.744 --> 00:05:09.384
Finish with an integrated dataset.

00:05:09.384 --> 00:05:12.304
At rest, cardiac output is five litres per minute.

00:05:13.184 --> 00:05:17.244
During a specified activity it is twelve litres per minute, an increase of

00:05:17.244 --> 00:05:21.164
seven litres per minute or one hundred forty percent relative to rest.

00:05:21.804 --> 00:05:24.544
The final value is two point four times baseline.

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:29.264
A simultaneous rise in ventilation is consistent with coordinated responses,

00:05:30.024 --> 00:05:34.624
but those two measurements alone do not identify every neural, hormonal or

00:05:34.624 --> 00:05:35.344
metabolic cause.

00:05:36.504 --> 00:05:39.984
Compare matched time points and state whether values are instantaneous or

00:05:39.984 --> 00:05:40.624
averaged.

00:05:41.104 --> 00:05:44.304
A recovery measurement is not the same as one taken during activity.

00:05:45.124 --> 00:05:49.164
Use replicated observations and control relevant conditions before claiming

00:05:49.164 --> 00:05:52.104
that one changed factor caused the entire response.
